Completion:
assistant
<think>Okay, so I have this piecewise function f(x) defined in two parts: when x is less than 7, it uses x squared minus 4, and when x is greater than or equal to 7, it uses x minus 13. And I need to find f(f(f(17))). Hmm, nested functions can sometimes trip me up, but let me take it step by step.

First, let me tackle the innermost part: f(17). Since we start there, I just need to figure out which piece of the function applies when x = 17.

Looking at the conditions: if x < 7 use x² - 4; otherwise (which includes equal to or greater than 7), use x - 13. Well, 17 is definitely greater than or equal to 7, right? Yep, way greater. So I should use the second part: x - 13.

Plugging in x = 17 into that: f(17) = 17 - 13 = 4. Alright, got that first result: f(17) = 4.

Now, moving out one layer, I need to find f(f(17)) which simplifies to f(4), since we just found that f(17) = 4.

Again, apply f to x = 4 now. Check which condition holds for x = 4: since 4 < 7, we take the first part of the piecewise function: x² - 4.

Calculating that: f(4) = (4)² - 4 = 16 - 4 = 12. Wait, hold on, is that right? Let me verify: yes, squaring 4 gives 16, minus 4 gives 12. Okay, that seems straightforward.

Now we move to the outermost layer: we need to compute f(f(f(17))) which now requires us to find f(12), since f(f(17)) = f(4) = 12.

Again, applying f to x = 12: determine which part of the piecewise function to use.

Compare x = 12 against 7: since 12 is greater than or equal to 7 (in fact, much greater), we again use the second part: x - 13.

Therefore, f(12) = 12 - 13 = -1.

